    "title": "CASAmigos",
    "year": "2025–2025",
    "detailed_description": "<h2>Overview</h2><p>This was an end of term group project for my masters in Connected Environments: Data Visualization class.<br/>We were given a seemingly simple prompt: Build a data device and create it’s digital twin to convey some data. <br/>In search of inspiration we started with rough concepts for the data device. Early in the course one of our professors, introduced us to his obsession with gauges, and how such a seemingly simple device can be used to very easily convey information to users. One such gauge he had discussed was using plastic syringes to push and pull water from a tube. <br/>This one immediately caught our attention, and we set about trying to find a place to fit something so contradicting as an electronics project that uses water.</p><h2>Solution</h2><p>The moment the idea landed, it was one of those face-palm instants; painfully obvious in hindsight, completely non-obvious until someone said it out loud. We&#x27;d been circling around concepts for weeks: river levels, hydroelectric data, usage statistics. All fine. All forgettable. Then someone said: if we&#x27;re trying to visualize water, why aren&#x27;t we using water?</p><p>The room went quiet for a beat. Then everyone started talking at once.</p><p>There was something almost comedic about it, we were in a building stuffed with sophisticated sensors and cutting-edge technology, and the breakthrough insight was to use syringes and a tube. But that&#x27;s often how the best ideas work. The constraint becomes the concept. The medium becomes the message. If the data is about water, the display should feel like water, should move like water, should make you viscerally aware that water is the thing being consumed. </p><h3>Research</h3><p>By this point we had a high level understanding of what to do:</p><ul><li>Grab the live data</li><li>Store it for additional analysis</li><li>Create a gauge using these syringes</li><li>Create a digital twin in Unity</li><li>Create a dashboard in Unity</li><li>Link the three components together (Data Device, Data Aggregate, Digital Twin)</li></ul><p>My portion of the project centered around the data aggregation step, while also filling in gaps for my teammates where I could.</p><h3>System Architecture</h3><p>As I dug through the MQTT data stream with our mentor, it soon became apparent that it showed the total water usage from the time the sensors were first powered on. This indicated a level of unreliability from the sensor output. A sensor installed on day 1 of the building being powered on, versus a sensor being installed on day 5, would show vastly different numbers. Additionally, since it is an MQTT stream, we don’t get an understanding of historical data, only the immediate reading. To solve this, I created a javascript that ran every 10 minutes, to store the values from the MQTT stream in a SQL database along with a timestamp, floor number and location of sensor. Using this database, I created an API with 6 endpoints for the other two teams to access. The script and the database was saved on a raspberry pi, and automated using cronjob to poll every 10 minutes. This provided a method to grab historical data, and normalize all of it, so water usage levels could be better compared.</p><figure><img src=\"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/jwneocyf/production/f43f4938f81d509af04519aafc09ce6f80ecc3d6-701x642.png?w=800&q=85&auto=format\" alt=\"\" /><figcaption>System Architecture</figcaption></figure><h3>Testing and Integration</h3><p>As my portion of the project involved frequent readings and aggregation of the data, I started with manually running my scripts to grab data by each floor, and watching them populate the SQL table. Once I had the information in the table, I used <a href=\"https://www.postman.com/\">Postman</a> to query the endpoints to verify the JSON packets. After that it was just a matter of setting up the cronjob, and monitoring the data every few hours, and making sure the Raspberry Pi’s memory still had space.</p><h3>Final Thoughts</h3><p>CASAmigos taught me something I&#x27;ve carried into every project since: the most powerful data visualizations aren&#x27;t the ones with the most information. They&#x27;re the ones that make you feel something. Numbers on a dashboard tell you what&#x27;s happening. Water rising in a syringe makes you think about what it means.&quot;</p>",
