    "title": "Gesture Recognizer",
    "year": "2024–2024",
    "detailed_description": "<h2>Overview</h2><p>The goal of this project was to create something that could leverage deep learning models with Edge Impulse, and run it on a Arduino Nano 33 BLE. </p><p>Throughout my Masters program, we spent a lot of time in 3D modeling software like Autodesk Inventor and OnShape. Whipping through models with a mouse and keyboard shortcuts is thrilling, but it keeps reminding me of the scene from Iron Man 1. Where Tony directly interacts with the wireframe model designed by J.A.R.V.I.S. While designing some motion tracker and laser wire projectors would be cool, that would’ve definitely been out of scope. Instead I sought inspiration from Tony’s action, and one of the coolest gaming controllers that never really worked. The Nintendo PowerGlove. Imagine having a glove that lets you manipulate your models in real time. Rotation, grab and pan, extrude, cut a hole through, measure, and place. The power of CAD at the pads of your fingers.</p><h2>Research</h2><p>Starting off with the famed PowerGlove I found an <a href=\"https://electronics.howstuffworks.com/nintendo-power-glove.htm\">article</a> that highlighted how it worked. I knew I wanted to track how the users fingers moved, but also rotation was important. Between finger movements and rotation tracking, I could do the basic CAD movement interactions. To prove this is a viable product, I simplified the actions I wanted to target: fist, thumbs up, point. In addition, the Arduino had a built in accelerometer which was used for rotation tracking.</p><h2>Solution</h2><p>With the sensors and microcontrollers selected, the prototying process began; and what a process that was. It kicked off with me learning how to sew. I’ve watched youtube videos about it, and thankfully the people at UCL’s Institute of Making were of great help! They helped me plan out the sewing patterns, and sort out measurements. The gloves consisted of “pockets” for each of the flex sensors on the thumb, index, and middle fingers. Along with a 3D printed plate that sat on the top on the hand for the arduino to sit in. </p><figure><img src=\"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/jwneocyf/production/03dd22573ca30d678f3307d59db450b4ef9d1805-4080x3072.jpg?w=800&q=85&auto=format\" alt=\"\" /><figcaption>First Glove Design</figcaption></figure><p>The training setup was pretty simple, once the hardware was assembled, the Edge Impulse Data Forwarder was used to pipe the data to Edge Impulse for sampling. The samples were taken by recording 30 seconds of a repeated action, then split in the Edge Impulse GUI into multiple samples.</p><h3>Closing Thoughts</h3><p>The moment I&#x27;m most proud of from this project isn&#x27;t when the model first classified a gesture correctly (though that was deeply satisfying). It&#x27;s the moment I held the finished glove on my hand, made a fist, and thought about a designer sitting in front of a 3D model, rotating it with a twist of their wrist, extruding a surface with a pinch, cutting a hole with a point. The technology works. The pipeline from sensor to inference to output is real and running on a device the size of a postage stamp. What comes next is just design. Figuring out what gestures feel natural, what feedback tells you the system understood you, what the glove needs to become before someone would actually want to wear it all day. That&#x27;s the problem I want to keep solving.</p>",
