    "title": "Lumos",
    "year": "2024–2024",
    "detailed_description": "<h2>Overview</h2><p>The first time someone picked up the wand and swept it across the portal, they stopped mid-motion. The light changed. They looked at the wall, then back at the wand, then back at the wall. Then they did it again. Slower this time, more deliberately. Watching a single node shift from white to deep amber as they moved. There was no explanation needed. They just started playing.</p><p>That moment was the whole point. We&#x27;d been given a technical challenge: build a controller for a 52-node light wall installation, and from the very first conversation, I knew I didn&#x27;t want to build another app. Everybody has an app. I wanted to build something you could hold in your hand and wave, something that felt less like configuring a system and more like conducting one.</p><figure><img src=\"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/jwneocyf/production/acfbd75834a5d2849006da02042f181ee2195286-3072x4080.jpg?rect=0,198,3072,3645&w=800&q=85&auto=format\" alt=\"Lumos in Use\" /><figcaption>A flick of the wrist</figcaption></figure><h3>Research</h3><p>The inspiration came from thinking about colour itself. Every colour you can see is just a combination of three values: red, green, and blue. Three values. Three axes. Three magnetometers. If I put each one at a point on a circle and let a user move a magnet through that space, they could physically inhabit the colour wheel, not tap it on a screen, but move through it in three dimensions. The wand wasn&#x27;t an input device. It was an instrument.</p><h2>Solution</h2><p>The circle idea itself made a lot of sense however. From doing more research on the color wheel, and other ways to allow users the maximum functionality, the color wheel is objectively the solution with the most options. By pulling this concept from screens to the real world, I knew I should start with something circular, and by adding dimension to it (like a sphere of influence), I can add even more variation such as light intensity. Reading deeper into how we interpret colors digitally, it’s obvious that the RGB value is key, and this is where my history working with magnetometers comes into play. If we use a circle to represent all the possible colors, and we know that all the colors can be represented by these 3 values, each of these maxims (max red, max green, max blue) can be a magnetometer. By moving a magnet within this space, I can create any color within this wheel.</p><figure><img src=\"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/jwneocyf/production/79e1bbc4eba1ec0c71f156cddd793b2515295c82-3072x4080.jpg?rect=0,1236,2651,1893&w=800&q=85&auto=format\" alt=\"Light Wall\" /><figcaption>Light Wall Installation</figcaption></figure><p>The Light Wall installation however had 52 individually accessible light nodes. To speak to any one node, I would need to send an MQTT message to a topic with the Node ID listed. If a user wanted to select a specific light node, there needed to be some kind of method to select it. This is where the rotary knob came in. The initial idea was to have a button that allowed users to manually select a node, I figured adding some rotation would thematically tie into circles, and add an extra tactile feel to the device. To build this, I studied this <a href=\"https://www.instructables.com/Hackable-Rotary-Control-With-Built-in-Display-a-Te/\">instructable</a> that deconstructs a rotary timer. Using this as a basis for design, I took to OnShape, and redesigned it my way with a rotary encoder at the center, and a LED screen baked into the center to display which node was being selected. By rotating the knob the user would be able to select all the way up till node 52, and use the “portal” of magnetometers to adjust color live.</p><figure><img src=\"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/jwneocyf/production/1270377bca2546567e86b805e5577a2d9a65d341-3072x4080.jpg?rect=59,943,2887,2579&w=800&q=85&auto=format\" alt=\"wheel selector\" /><figcaption>Wheel Selector</figcaption></figure><p>As these items by themselves were technically interesting, I leveraged the action of “waving a wand” to cohesively bring it together. By &quot;swiss-cheesing” the portal, I was able to easily run wires through the portal in a way users wouldn’t notice, and by using a model of a hand from TinkerCAD, I was able to hide the wires going into the base box giving everything a clean and wireless look. </p><figure><img src=\"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/jwneocyf/production/d7923eb44d23624f681d3d384bf67606989ddcd0-969x1287.jpg?w=800&q=85&auto=format\" alt=\"Wires winding around the portal\" /><figcaption>Swiss Cheesed Part for Wire Management</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/jwneocyf/production/39ea67bf63b5f55e6356f00c1026e3c6b1068151-1709x1287.jpg?w=800&q=85&auto=format\" alt=\"\" /><figcaption>Hiding the Wires in the Hand</figcaption></figure><h3>System Architecture</h3><p>As the light nodes required MQTT messages to communicate, an Arduino MKR WiFi 1010 was used to coordinate everything. The TLV493D magnetometers use I2C to communicate, but have their I2C addresses soldered. Therefore to communicate to all 3, I used a TCA9548A multiplexer to communicate to each sensor. A rolling average was applied to the readings from these magnetometers to smooth out the noisy readings. </p><figure><img src=\"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/jwneocyf/production/b1d2799e032fc4f3fc83df32d4c7407325fb8871-711x301.png?w=800&q=85&auto=format\" alt=\"\" /><figcaption>Architecture Diagram</figcaption></figure><h2>Technical Challenges</h2><p>Lumos lives or dies on feel. The rolling average smoothing wasn&#x27;t just a signal processing decision, it was the difference between a wand that felt responsive and one that felt twitchy and broken. Every technical choice fed back into the experience. That&#x27;s the design constraint I find most interesting: when the engineering is in service of a feeling.</p>",
